name: the signing chain
after: 30-root
intro: The last level hashed the filesystem under your feet and matched it to a
intro: number on the kernel command line. But a number is only worth the thing
intro: that vouches for it. This mission climbs the other half of the chain --
intro: the half above the root hash, the half you cannot see with sha256sum --
intro: and, just as important, learns exactly where the chain stops. Four links,
intro: each checked by the one above it:
intro:
intro:   firmware secure boot, using keys YOU enrolled
intro:     verifies the signed UKI -- kernel and command line in one signed file
intro:       which carries the verity root hash and the root's PARTUUID
intro:         which covers every block of the read-only squashfs
intro:
intro: No payloads, no tools you do not have. Just the argument, followed up.
---
setup: The root hash you trusted did not float in on its own -- it rode in on
setup: the kernel command line, and that command line is baked INTO a single
setup: signed file, the UKI, on the EFI partition. Edit the hash and you break
setup: the signature over the whole image. That is why a checksum stored beside
setup: the data it describes proves nothing, but this one proves everything.
q: print the command line the firmware handed the kernel -- the signed line the root hash rides in
a: cat /proc/cmdline
ref: cat
teach: cat
---
setup: That UKI is signed with keys you enrolled into the firmware yourself:
setup: the platform key PK, the key-exchange key KEK, and the signature db. They
setup: go in in an order that matters -- because enrolling ONE of them is the
setup: act that takes the firmware out of setup mode and seals the other two
setup: against further change. Get that order wrong and you can brick a laptop.
q: which of those three keys is enrolled LAST, sealing the rest
a: PK
a: pk
a: the platform key
hint: two letters -- until it lands, the firmware is still in setup mode
---
setup: A UKI is a PE binary, and PE signatures have a quirk you must know before
setup: you ever reach for sha256sum to check one. Authenticode does NOT hash the
setup: whole file: it carves out three regions -- the checksum field, the pointer
setup: to the certificate table, and the signature blob itself -- so a file can
setup: carry the signature of its own remaining bytes.
q: how many regions of the PE does authenticode exclude from its digest
a: 3
a: three
hint: the checksum, the cert-table pointer, and the signature
---
setup: So a plain sha256sum of the signed UKI will not equal the digest the
setup: firmware verified -- they hash different byte ranges. This is the single
setup: sharpest trap in the whole chain: the obvious tool gives the wrong number
setup: and it is not lying to you, it is answering a different question.
q: name the digest the firmware actually checks -- authenticode, or sha256sum
a: authenticode
hint: the PE-aware one that skips three regions, not the whole-file one
---
setup: Now revocation. A signature proves WHO signed an image. It can never, by
setup: itself, prove WHEN -- so a key stolen today can sign something and backdate
setup: the story. The firmware's answer is a denylist of digests it refuses even
setup: though they were once validly signed.
q: name that denylist the firmware consults to reject a still-signed but known-bad image
a: dbx
hint: the counterpart of db -- the forbidden-signatures database
---
setup: Verified boot has now proven one thing precisely: the machine runs what
setup: was SIGNED. It has said nothing about whether what was signed matches the
setup: source you can actually read. xos closes that last gap by building the
setup: same bytes every time -- rebuild from source, get the same image.sha256,
setup: and the signature you trusted now vouches for code, not just for a blob.
q: one word: the property that makes what-was-signed equal what-is-in-the-source
a: reproducibility
a: reproducible
a: reproducible builds
hint: rebuild from source, compare image.sha256, get the same number
---
setup: Know where the chain STOPS, or you will trust it too far. The signature
setup: covers the kernel, the command line and every block of the root. Two
setup: things on the stick it does not cover: the partition table, and the
setup: filesystem metadata of the EFI partition. Tamper there and you can stop
setup: the machine booting -- you cannot change what runs.
q: name the filesystem whose metadata on the efi partition sits outside the signature
a: FAT
a: fat
a: vfat
hint: three letters, the classic efi-partition filesystem
---
setup: That last gap has a physical answer, not a cryptographic one.
q: what feature on the stick itself closes even the unsigned-metadata gap
a: write-protect switch
a: a write-protect switch
a: write protect switch
a: the write-protect switch
hint: a switch on the stick that makes the whole device read-only in hardware
---
setup: And the limit no design on this stick can cross: the host's own silicon.
setup: A malicious BIOS or an SMM implant on the machine you plugged into reads
setup: your keystrokes no matter how perfect your chain is. xos defends against
setup: the host's installed operating system and its disks -- not its hardware.
q: name the host layer, beneath its os, that no stick can defend against
a: firmware
a: the firmware
a: BIOS
a: bios
a: SMM
hint: the code that runs before any operating system does
---
setup: Close the loop. You have followed the chain from the firmware down; now
setup: read the bottom link one more time -- the anchored number every link above
setup: exists to protect.
q: pull the verity root hash out of the signed command line, and nothing else
a: sed -n 's/.*sha256 \([0-9a-f]\{64\}\).*/\1/p' /proc/cmdline
ref: sed
teach: sed -n
hint: the 64 hex characters after the word sha256
